    expectedAnswer: "Repository Pattern isolates data-access details behind an interface. The application depends on the contract while the implementation can use REST, GraphQL, IndexedDB, a database, or a test double.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the API concept.\n\nStep 1 — Define the data contract first.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface User {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n}\n\ninterface UserRepository {\n  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;\n}\n\nclass MemoryUserRepository implements UserRepository {\n  private readonly users = new Map<string, User>();\n\n  constructor(seed: readonly User[]) {\n    for (const user of seed) {\n      this.users.set(user.id, user);\n    }\n  }\n\n  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {\n    return this.users.get(id) ?? null;\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / Map-backed usage:\n```ts\ninterface User {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n}\n\ninterface UserRepository {\n  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;\n}\n\nclass MemoryUserRepository implements UserRepository {\n  private readonly users = new Map<string, User>();\n\n  constructor(seed: readonly User[]) {\n    for (const user of seed) {\n      this.users.set(user.id, user);\n    }\n  }\n\n  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {\n    return this.users.get(id) ?? null;\n  }\n}\n\nasync function example(): Promise<User | null> {\n  const repository = new MemoryUserRepository([\n    { id: \"1\", name: \"Rasik\" },\n  ]);\n\n  return repository.findById(\"1\");\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nA service can depend on `UserRepository` while tests inject `MemoryUserRepository`.\n\nComplexity:\nMap lookup is O(1) average.\n\nTrade-off:\nRepositories reduce coupling to storage but can become unnecessary abstractions over a trivial API client.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the API boundary, failure behavior, security implications, performance, and why the chosen approach fits the system.",
