    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the API concept.\n\nStep 1 — Model an authenticated principal.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface Principal {\n  userId: string;\n  roles: string[];\n}\n\ninterface Authenticator {\n  authenticate(token: string): Promise<Principal | null>;\n}\n\nasync function requireUser(\n  auth: Authenticator,\n  token: string,\n): Promise<Principal> {\n  const principal = await auth.authenticate(token);\n\n  if (principal === null) {\n    throw new Error(\"Unauthorized\");\n  }\n\n  return principal;\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / role lookup:\n```ts\ninterface Principal {\n  userId: string;\n  roles: string[];\n}\n\nfunction hasRole(\n  principal: Principal,\n  role: string,\n): boolean {\n  return principal.roles.includes(role);\n}\n\nconst canManage = hasRole(\n  { userId: \"1\", roles: [\"admin\"] },\n  \"admin\",\n);\n```\n\nExample:\nRequest → authenticate credential → build principal → authorize operation → execute use case.\n\nComplexity:\nRole lookup is O(n) in the number of roles.\n\nTrade-off:\nCentral authentication simplifies identity handling, but authorization must remain explicit at every protected operation.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the API boundary, failure behavior, security implications, performance, and why the chosen approach fits the system.",
