    expectedAnswer: "Retry repeats a failed operation when the failure is likely transient. Safe retry design requires bounded attempts, backoff, idempotency, and awareness that some failures should never be retried.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the API concept.\n\nStep 1 — Retry only a known-safe operation.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface RetryOptions {\n  attempts: number;\n}\n\nasync function retry<T>(\n  operation: () => Promise<T>,\n  options: RetryOptions,\n): Promise<T> {\n  let lastError: unknown;\n\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < options.attempts; attempt++) {\n    try {\n      return await operation();\n    } catch (error) {\n      lastError = error;\n    }\n  }\n\n  throw lastError;\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / Promise helper:\n```ts\nasync function once(): Promise<string> {\n  return Promise.resolve(\"success\");\n}\n\nasync function example(): Promise<string> {\n  return once();\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nRetry a GET request after a temporary network timeout, but do not blindly retry a payment POST without idempotency protection.\n\nComplexity:\nAt most O(attempts) operation executions.\n\nTrade-off:\nRetries improve resilience but can amplify load and latency during outages.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the API boundary, failure behavior, security implications, performance, and why the chosen approach fits the system.",
