    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the API concept.\n\nStep 1 — Model a page explicitly.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface Page<T> {\n  items: T[];\n  page: number;\n  pageSize: number;\n  total: number;\n}\n\nfunction slicePage<T>(\n  items: readonly T[],\n  page: number,\n  pageSize: number,\n): Page<T> {\n  const start = (page - 1) * pageSize;\n\n  return {\n    items: items.slice(start, start + pageSize),\n    page,\n    pageSize,\n    total: items.length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / array helpers:\n```ts\nconst users = [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"];\n\nconst page = users.slice(2, 4);\n```\n\nExample:\n`GET /users?page=3&pageSize=20` returns a bounded response. Cursor APIs can instead return `nextCursor`.\n\nComplexity:\nArray slicing is O(pageSize) for the returned page.\n\nTrade-off:\nOffset pagination is easy to understand; cursor pagination is usually more stable for rapidly changing ordered datasets.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the API boundary, failure behavior, security implications, performance, and why the chosen approach fits the system.",
